      "content" : "• it has precisely one positive eigenvalue and r − 1 negative eigenvalues.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 573.602, 540.002, 605.469 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We denote the set of all Mandelstam matrices of rank r by Mn,r. This is a semialgebraic set in R("
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 98.487, 579.275, 112.331, 592.023 ],
    "font" : "CMR6",
    "font size" : 5.978,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "n+1 2"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 561.176, 540.001, 591.864 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "), the space of all symmetric matrices. The following is the Mandelstam analogue of the familiar characterization of positive semidefinite matrices in terms of principal minors."
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 15,
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 129.793, 540.001, 549.033 ],
    "numbering style" : "arabic numbers",
    "number of list items" : 2,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 501.89, 540.0, 549.033 ],
      "font" : "CMBX12",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Lemma 2.1. A symmetric n × n matrix S is Mandelstam if and only if (−1)|I|−1 det(SI) ≥ 0 for all I ⊆ [n], (4)",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 1,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 483.18, 299.269, 495.932 ],
        "font" : "CMTI12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "where det(SI) are the principal minors of S."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 2,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 415.434, 540.005, 471.524 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Proof. This follows from the general results in [6]. We refer to Baker’s exposition in [3]. The key step is Cauchy’s interlacing theorem [12]. This states that the eigenvalues of SI interlace the eigenvalues of SJ whenever I ⊂ J. Hence, if SI has at most one positive eigenvalue then so does SJ. But SJ cannot have all negative eigenvalues because its trace is non-negative."
      }, {
        "type" : "table",
        "pdfua_tag" : "Table",
        "id" : 3,
        "level" : "3",
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 531.538, 419.22, 539.358, 427.122 ],
        "number of rows" : 1,
        "number of columns" : 1,
        "rows" : [ {
          "type" : "table row",
          "row number" : 1,
          "cells" : [ {
            "type" : "table cell",
            "pdfua_tag" : "TD",
            "page number" : 1,
            "bounding box" : [ 531.936, 419.42, 538.96, 426.923 ],
            "row number" : 1,
            "column number" : 1,
            "row span" : 1,
            "column span" : 1,
            "kids" : [ ]
          } ]
        } ]
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 4,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 334.239, 540.001, 404.774 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "The name of our matrices refers to the physicist Stanley Mandelstam (1928–2016) who is credited for introducing the variables sij in the context of scattering amplitudes. In [14] the role of Mn,r as a kinematic space is recognized. A term more familiar to mathematicians might be “Lorentzian matrices.” These encode Lorentzian quadratic forms [5, 6]. We here use the term Lorentzian matrix for a Mandelstam matrix whose entries sij are all non-negative."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 5,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 294.667, 539.998, 331.589 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Mandelstam matrices arise as Gram matrices of momentum vectors in R1+d with the Lorentzian inner product. A non-zero momentum vector is any vector p ∈ R1+d of the form"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 6,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 252.503, 275.957, 540.0, 288.709 ],
        "font" : "CMMI12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "p = λ(1,x1,...,xd), (5)"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 7,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 221.45, 540.0, 264.341 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "for some scalar λ ̸= 0, and x = (x1,...,xd) in the closed unit ball Bd = {x ∈ Rd : ||x|| ≤ 1}. Given n momentum vectors, p(i), their Gram matrix S = [sij] has entries sij = p(i) · p(j). This is the matrix in (1). The entries of S may now be written as"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 8,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 160.172, 540.0, 208.964 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "sij = λiλj 1 − ⟨x(i),x(j)⟩ (6) Here · is the Lorentz inner product on R1+d and ⟨ , ⟩ is the Euclidean inner product on Rd."
      } ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
